ealth care systems still  need to evolve in order to be able to  fully realize this true potential this  is a this is a comic strip about the  implementation of PCR at the point of  care for respiratory viruses and in the  discussion that these two lovely figures  are having isn't thinking about the flow  of the dollars and how they really  demonstrate value um the first book  little man here says let's see capital  expense consumable service contract  what's this I'm figuring about how much  it would cost to do point-of-care PCR  for respiratory viruses sigh it's a big  increase in the lab budget so the other  one has to I do it early results could  lead to improvements in patient care and  overall healthcare savings that's a  great thing however the administration  says the lab cost has to be offset by  budget cuts healthcare savings are hard  to measure and may not be in the silo of  the lab budget pill it to the kid with  the cost so it's a really great  illustration of the fact that we still  sometimes struggle with the way that  healthcare systems are set up and  they're set up in a very siloed way and  within those silos oftentimes an  increase in the lab budget from  implementing a better test even though  it's resulting in savings somewhere else  in the healthcare system it can be very  difficult to have that conversation and  I think that that's really the best  point as we evolved into this new future  I think we're going to see more and more  health systems evolve to be able to have  this conversation at the same time it's  really important to recognize that not  all health systems are there today so in  order to really drive these types of  changes you really need to understand  your stakeholders and you also really  need to understand their pressure points  for example within the healthcare system  you can sometimes think about a payer as  if a payer can get engaged with a  project you want to do and if a pair  believes it can have value that can help  to put pressure on an overall healthcare  system to want to drive that change as  well so in summary the evidence required  to achieve regulatory approval is step  one it's not our final destination and  we still have a long way to do go how a  test could change clinical care if  theoretically utilized in a certain way  is no longer compelling to support value  we have to be demonstrating clinical  evidence value in the real world and we  oftentimes need to be demonstrating it  again and again to show the constant  value lastly implementation is really  hard implementation is sort of the dirty  work right that it's not it's not as fun  to go through the Nitty Gritty of  figuring out how to actually make  something work in a real-world setting  but if you're going to do it  successfully  you really have to understand the  different stakeholders their interests  what motivates them and you also really  need to understand the specific  situation you cannot have a  one-size-fits-all solution what worked  one time and implementation might not  work in another part of your institution  because it's a different set of  stakeholders we're going to think about  things in a different way and with that  I'd like to take lab I'd like to thank  lab roots for the opportunity to give  this presentation thank you thank you to  dr. Elizabeth Marlowe  and Joanna Sickler for your informative  presentations I would also like to thank  leverets for making today's educational  webcast possible as a final reminder our  speakers will follow up with any  questions you've submitted via email  that's all for now thank you for joining  us we'll see you next time goodbye

